#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171823 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] :question/id #uuid "bccee2b9-f937-5df5-b3cf-4e45096c9ac3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9465" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf A(x,y) = \\sqrt{x}\\,\\mathbf i + y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf B(x,y,z) = \\ln y\\,\\mathbf i - (x+y)\\,\\mathbf j  + z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf C(t) = \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf i + (2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf A(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\:|\\: x \\geq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$  It assigns points in $\\mathbb{R}$ (not $\\mathbb{R^2}$) to vectors in $\\mathbb{R^2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171824 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "193765c8-ea2c-5d90-b902-09d6d44a3a67" :question/math-academy-id "e-9466" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\sqrt{1+x-y} \\, \\mathbf{j}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = \\sqrt{1+x-y} .\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf F\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad x=y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_1$ is the entire plane $\\mathbb R^2$ with the origin removed, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/d4a418bc0dbfc61c0e55c494db51ee0fa8125b6b7056ccbe4e32ddca82574dc2.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or zero. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1+x - y \\geq 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y\\leq 1 + x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_2,$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/242903cc94103f8bb9cdc190a229fe7e86395f44600e8318d728fea3bc339385.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf F$ is given by \n\n$$\nD =  D_1\\cap D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq 0,\\, y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/f02ca21443a81cdb039a3d97bab0b0f1036219bb89ecbdaa32386cbc4e5da6e3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171825 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] :question/id #uuid "61c3506a-5e4e-557f-bb8c-077170e93204" :question/math-academy-id "e-9468" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\n- $\\mathbf R(x,y,z) = 2\\,\\mathbf i - x\\,\\mathbf j + y\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf S(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i - y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf T(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{z}\\,\\mathbf i - \\dfrac{y}{z}\\,\\mathbf j + \\dfrac{z}{2}\\,\\mathbf k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf R(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z) \\in \\mathbb{R^3}$ to a vector in $\\mathbb{R^3}.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf S(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $\\mathbf T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\:|\\: z \\neq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e085e56c-ab04-5255-8f2e-e486bf529732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318343} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8a7227-1927-51df-b1cd-eaa236149836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318344} {:answer/id #uuid "01f45662-f409-5414-a3a4-14a855652d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318345} {:answer/id #uuid "66bb3258-d9ab-5a7a-a63f-c101903168af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318346} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe0d6ca-418d-5609-8c74-0abb8404b00d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66bb3258-d9ab-5a7a-a63f-c101903168af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318346} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5ae6d6-1977-505a-8208-c6ef97d6f3e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2d1a20d-340e-5980-ad18-92b97e0270ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-122465" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $G(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- $H(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{xy}\\,j$\n- $K(x,y,z)=x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1375d472-1f3e-5d83-b50f-685c2d76c8e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318349} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a52c9f-2a67-54fb-b9cd-66160388ccb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318350} {:answer/id #uuid "04b6017e-effd-58e9-a983-338f6df666f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318351} {:answer/id #uuid "b18e55a3-ca3f-54a2-aecc-f3d5701696d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318352} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fd84d5-1fa6-59d3-a072-71e74f79bd6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b18e55a3-ca3f-54a2-aecc-f3d5701696d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318352} :answer-field/id #uuid "74a80994-5d03-5b5b-b339-7b3f1ef81f02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "060c20fc-1491-5624-9b50-89ab0dfe4d22" :question/math-academy-id "q-122467" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $G(t)={e}^{t}\\,i-t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$\n- $H(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- $K(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3f51021-bc37-512e-9007-b21321c31ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/q-122518-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168482} {:answer/id #uuid "d06346a3-0f5d-5c1a-b0fb-ffcbff145ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/q-122518-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168483} {:answer/id #uuid "078b30e7-95e6-50ae-b28e-39e6d1dbe152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/q-122518-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168484} {:answer/id #uuid "221365d6-2e30-5cb0-9160-554e42fe2c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/q-122518-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168485} {:answer/id #uuid "3a7dc72d-7c5e-54b4-a84c-f33fc63d4bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/q-122518-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3f51021-bc37-512e-9007-b21321c31ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/q-122518-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168482} :answer-field/id #uuid "50b7f088-1715-51b5-abd9-89cc5a403807" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46b9e86b-e188-5e02-ada4-a07528592166" :question/math-academy-id "q-122518" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/e54618f7e72d70f299686ca2846a1d6fa0caccb8daecb090b3a653271c88fcd3.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${R}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172215 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "812cb85b-96a5-597e-9768-128851a5e3eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168494} {:answer/id #uuid "949d2205-8bc4-5692-b4f7-babe07661c45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168495} {:answer/id #uuid "0303117a-7361-5307-91c0-5bb718658463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168496} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb1d537-0855-5e8c-90ea-e7f2fc90b3d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168497} {:answer/id #uuid "35dfad6e-57fc-5fe4-9984-28cb82b15113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0303117a-7361-5307-91c0-5bb718658463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168496} :answer-field/id #uuid "00e5a510-8833-50f0-a6d1-d8482e153aae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b23ed53-bb63-5272-af53-a6429d08e4c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122643" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,i+(x+y)^{2}\\,j$\n- $Q(x,y,z)=y\\,i-xy\\,j+(z+1)\\,k$\n- $R(t)=(t+1)\\,i-(3t-1)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $R(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0140076f-dbce-5e24-9ef5-2bdd8ea250f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318356} {:answer/id #uuid "26f5b60d-77e8-52a2-9ec8-a4771bc1be93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318357} {:answer/id #uuid "45225818-e430-5673-aa86-4ee148f60012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318358} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7de542-d8b3-58c4-ae51-2e845039e3ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318359} {:answer/id #uuid "6023ce95-d388-57be-a90a-cc3d4edd1b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f7de542-d8b3-58c4-ae51-2e845039e3ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318359} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7461103-a9fb-5455-8f8e-6b4f589dd359" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77b23b1c-02b1-5f1f-b4d9-170c31cf7032" :question/math-academy-id "q-122650" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $U(x,y)=⟨\\frac{1}{x+2},\\sqrt{x-y}⟩$\n- $V(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},1-y⟩$\n- $W(x,y,z)=⟨x,-y,-z⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne -2,\\,x-y\\ge 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $W(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff6da96-05a5-532c-85a5-cc5fdf1b92a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318363} {:answer/id #uuid "a29c1389-354a-5f37-9781-03b858b563e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318364} {:answer/id #uuid "768a89b8-6647-5a6c-81fa-f88e2a38b2b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318365} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9c861d-81d5-57f5-9b7f-52d429381913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318366} {:answer/id #uuid "48ff8ded-4323-51b5-b974-fc471c4b1e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a29c1389-354a-5f37-9781-03b858b563e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318364} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0c08d0d-9def-515c-9953-db6a6492384e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04610650-408c-5968-86aa-9a134cc6b4b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-122653" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $A(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y),\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)⟩$\n- $B(x,y,z)=⟨-2x,3{y}^{3},{z}^{5}⟩$\n- $C(t)=⟨2-{t}^{2},{e}^{t-1}⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x+y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$  It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1edc043e-6c4a-5c51-9d5a-c05022a6c623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318370} {:answer/id #uuid "925ceec8-7bcd-539f-815c-b30471d5c0a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x<y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318371} {:answer/id #uuid "e89f0acb-eee4-516f-bd2c-c08c525da7ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318372} {:answer/id #uuid "437b42cc-62f8-5a34-8633-9ec5fd34964e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318373} {:answer/id #uuid "2a751cf7-b090-5131-9034-1d832dd3ae47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0),\\,x\\ge y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e89f0acb-eee4-516f-bd2c-c08c525da7ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318372} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fadc5fa-aac6-5fc0-ab2f-e0d4b0c47961" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1db4c001-2e6a-51e7-a72a-6deb28c9845a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122657" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,i+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y),\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-y>0\\,⟹\\,x>y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>y}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2af2b156-a85d-5148-82e4-bf8728c27825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x<{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318377} {:answer/id #uuid "47eb46c9-fd73-53f8-9965-fc87ec66d71c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318378} {:answer/id #uuid "40bd274f-2d18-5617-8781-6c420660cb69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2},\\,x\\ne 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318379} {:answer/id #uuid "2ada3ef3-74e6-5b96-9a3c-fd5331ca6908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318380} {:answer/id #uuid "8a67d34f-901a-501a-be60-4032f2b91d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2},\\,x>1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ada3ef3-74e6-5b96-9a3c-fd5331ca6908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318380} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bd58f7c-d78d-5371-b618-29a0c1ca0373" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9d1b0c7-3a1c-573d-9729-ab5857b0ca01" :question/math-academy-id "q-122663" :question/problem "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{1/x}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-{y}^{2}),\\,Q(x,y)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{1/x}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the logarithm is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,x>{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator of the exponent vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x>{y}^{2}}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318382 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1abdd295-e1ca-510f-8906-63b8a402eb52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/4a15607520f8ef05913aab5d1724c8b3158457e56d455b6eb756707263494308.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318384} {:answer/id #uuid "4669ec38-146b-5557-91f9-d9f36767b087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/2469abf572b9b6fd9da28ae81951b99acdca9b97275af2bc0a96d3411d7fbea1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318385} {:answer/id #uuid "7c225d1b-4447-57fc-b3db-f4dc32fa8c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/0e6843335d08bc42a95a5801aa683aeb2a043df0cd7edc03ffbc36a9385fee2a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318386} {:answer/id #uuid "94535098-d9b2-597c-a7de-96c039db9728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/a3884061e6c5f8b5310a8b0bcf06ac844b12b418497d9f6e5b3beced6147d964.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318387} {:answer/id #uuid "b1173be2-eb62-5982-b322-c00e797dc1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/26709ab7fa3cf1962ff9dca9be510cf5c24f3568f82347a8349e77dc5095fb4c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4669ec38-146b-5557-91f9-d9f36767b087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/2469abf572b9b6fd9da28ae81951b99acdca9b97275af2bc0a96d3411d7fbea1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318385} :answer-field/id #uuid "01800486-e331-5a79-864a-6dc358d968fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6ea7f019-2997-5f2e-851c-1c905f5a74b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-122671" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/9f5f11a4ca31cb796f5a95a1541aa946e24fd1b7079ab8069e5743d41d92d764.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${R}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/95e8d137f90135de1727451fbc96ddfcc325e9e4461054615c91743a4a9ea54a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e166f35-7a38-52de-9f28-0f70455cfe48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/59ec29347dea9437c8217a208bde0a8b200e37fcbce9c223d0358011a0685297.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318391} {:answer/id #uuid "380933d6-b850-5fcd-994e-53ae99588b0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/cb10aa8f4d930ec7422da7dedfa0650244dceb01afe7cc849ed2bb16ffd3e44d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318392} {:answer/id #uuid "4cdfdd06-ce03-5b15-8de3-69d56dc49959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/a1ee85bca08b21494e6f1f47de3abcbd71d2d01117795ef91d8ee3e038b36271.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318393} {:answer/id #uuid "dcdaaea7-0f3c-5b2e-ba5a-3a86b326b0f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/27f151bdd3d619de0988b786e6b7517a7f7586844f9a7a35bf651063e7a7dfd1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318394} {:answer/id #uuid "67cd3ce8-95a2-55d0-aebd-f5fb05384bd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/fadd7ae69d155ebf23586b88369da8f6624b1919942dad543917592f2d3c195c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcdaaea7-0f3c-5b2e-ba5a-3a86b326b0f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/27f151bdd3d619de0988b786e6b7517a7f7586844f9a7a35bf651063e7a7dfd1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318394} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5902226-a782-5dc9-af05-70db43f2771a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e932ad34-5aaf-594d-8943-da2547f2acb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-122677" :question/problem "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-x}}\\,i+\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-x}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\ny-x>0\\,⟹\\,y>x.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/c78759bc8f9926159065129e00c1fc8b4341ab83ad40cc87e67a2e82b3e6b089.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2},$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/bdf02ff330f256327d0c9f1147c388b25184610b36ea7ab77bb2c570ec3be19a.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/0d3180a60e33c05e904c8fc00fdec4197510198fef3de3fa8c7b3549609205f6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "384a6e96-661d-5b22-bf7a-164e9ab6be82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318398} {:answer/id #uuid "41ab9b5d-2fd7-5aec-aee0-304e65d2805a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318399} {:answer/id #uuid "99d21c19-c99c-5f0b-bfa4-f42943df8126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318400} {:answer/id #uuid "37f7df0e-ae66-5127-9962-f5e0a94d8c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318401} {:answer/id #uuid "a11d1381-5cfe-5e01-adb6-d137bf5de30c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41ab9b5d-2fd7-5aec-aee0-304e65d2805a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318399} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f3a99d-28fc-555f-98b1-b751588c1cb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2651bc0d-cbe1-5684-a139-363dabaf9381" :question/math-academy-id "q-122678" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $U(x,y)=⟨(x+2){e}^{y},\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-y}⟩$\n- $V(x,y,z)=⟨5,{x}^{3}y,1-{y}^{3}⟩$\n- $W(x,y)=⟨x-3{y}^{2},2-y⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne y}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89add4a6-5ff3-53d9-afc5-058a99d1844b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318405} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f55209-3fea-5e26-ad98-fa69b297ccc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318406} {:answer/id #uuid "db29d631-be34-5b50-a380-b89a09958e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318407} {:answer/id #uuid "72504a1b-b300-5f93-9a76-900cbdbd4070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318408} {:answer/id #uuid "df5595ea-53e4-5d40-96b1-6bc094e5f3c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2f55209-3fea-5e26-ad98-fa69b297ccc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318406} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab7333a9-1370-5312-b061-1bad9cc26f55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9214bda-2e0f-56b0-8bb0-f583c0a525d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-122688" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $D(x,y,z)=5\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+(y+x)\\,k$\n- $E(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-\\sqrt{y}\\,j$\n- $G(x,y,z)=x(y+1)\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y-z)\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $D(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $G(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,y-z\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84ce8c8c-ed4a-5919-a5eb-6c30ab0870d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318412} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1a1200-ffe9-5670-ae34-a9f529c50e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318413} {:answer/id #uuid "ef403ee5-cc38-59ee-be6c-00010c48c7f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318414} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff1c7e4-2129-5eeb-af36-6a76093fbd4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318415} {:answer/id #uuid "a8640cf3-3e0d-55d6-96aa-007bfdde6237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84ce8c8c-ed4a-5919-a5eb-6c30ab0870d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318412} :answer-field/id #uuid "69f260d0-3b6c-5d58-b69f-8b9446f0d928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d426c0f2-275b-5420-ab2e-9119139f79d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-122694" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $N(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},\\sqrt{x+y}⟩$\n- $P(x,y,z)=⟨{e}^{x-2y},\\sqrt{3-x},3{z}^{3}⟩$\n- $Q(x,y,z)=⟨-1,2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-y+z)⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $P(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,3-x\\ge 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,2x-y+z>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73a141c2-1fbe-5ffc-8651-29e12a2c8c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318419} {:answer/id #uuid "03676bea-bacb-5503-b7dc-e4643ee902b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318420} {:answer/id #uuid "b74bf48e-ddd1-5a97-a19a-295712b7ee27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318421} {:answer/id #uuid "db125716-736b-5098-8776-d37b7cb0ca2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318422} {:answer/id #uuid "1b4e8f63-68f0-5398-b727-30054ba8b9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b74bf48e-ddd1-5a97-a19a-295712b7ee27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318421} :answer-field/id #uuid "131d98ff-795f-5c1d-9ad7-6408ed7d8dcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b9bc86f-94cc-5734-b961-e898de95133e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122696" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $C(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{x+y}$\n- $D(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3}-3,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y),7{x}^{3}⟩$\n- $E(x,y,z)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+z),\\operatorname{tan}⁡z,\\frac{1}{x-y}⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $C(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to functions in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $D(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne y,\\,z\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59117d6e-9073-527d-9d12-f6767cf467f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318425} {:answer/id #uuid "a13322c1-bd19-5745-9ce5-206071413be6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318426} {:answer/id #uuid "19c31f96-4471-53f1-bc27-9d77558def6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318427} {:answer/id #uuid "70456547-98e0-5aec-b06b-f9018a44b3f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318428} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4baf47-36a1-5bab-ae66-249a87a32d44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a13322c1-bd19-5745-9ce5-206071413be6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318426} :answer-field/id #uuid "34c2577f-83c1-5db0-a1f5-f859ca38ed77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4052d0d0-adca-5eb3-9bef-49295b1e5651" :question/math-academy-id "q-122697" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $U(x,y,z)=⟨x+2,\\sqrt{x-y},\\operatorname{ln}⁡z⟩$\n- $V(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},1-y⟩$\n- $W(t)=⟨{t}^{2},2t,2-3t⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ge y,\\,z>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $W(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
